\n1120 | \n1121 |             Use either ``mapper`` and ``axis`` to\n1122 |             specify the axis to target with ``mapper``, or ``index``\n1123 |             and/or ``columns``.\n1124 |         index : scalar, list-like, dict-like or function, optional\n1125 |             A scalar, list-like, dict-like or functions transformations to\n1126 |             apply to that axis' values.\n1127 |         columns : scalar, list-like, dict-like or function, optional\n1128 |             A scalar, list-like, dict-like or functions transformations to\n1129 |             apply to that axis' values.\n1130 |         axis : {0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns'}, default 0\n1131 |             The axis to rename.\n1132 |         copy : bool, default False\n1133 |             Also copy underlying data.\n1134 | \n1135 |             .. note::\n1136 |                 The `copy` keyword will change behavior in pandas 3.0.\n1137 |                 `Copy-on-Write\n1138 |                 <https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/user_guide/copy_on_write.html>`__\n1139 |                 will be enabled by default, which means that all methods with a\n1140 |                 `copy` keyword will use a lazy copy mechanism to defer the copy and\n1141 |                 ignore the `copy` keyword. The `copy` keyword will be removed in a\n1142 |                 future version of pandas.\n1143 | \n1144 |                 You can already get the future behavior and improvements through\n1145 |                 enabling copy on write ``pd.options.mode.copy_on_write = True``\n1146 | \n1147 |             .. deprecated:: 3.0.0\n1148 |         inplace : bool, default False\n1149 |             Modifies the object directly, instead of creating a new Series\n1150 |             or DataFrame.\n1151 | \n1152 |         Returns\n1153 |         -------\n1154 |         DataFrame, or None\n1155 |             The same type as the caller or None if ``inplace=True``.\n1156 | \n1157 |         See Also\n1158 |         --------\n1159 