use the ``~`` operator:\n5243 | \n5244 |         >>> ~s.isin([\"cow\", \"llama\"])\n5245 |         0    False\n5246 |         1    False\n5247 |         2    False\n5248 |         3     True\n5249 |         4    False\n5250 |         5     True\n5251 |         Name: animal, dtype: bool\n5252 | \n5253 |         Passing a single string as ``s.isin('llama')`` will raise an error. Use\n5254 |         a list of one element instead:\n5255 | \n5256 |         >>> s.isin([\"llama\"])\n5257 |         0     True\n5258 |         1    False\n5259 |         2     True\n5260 |         3    False\n5261 |         4     True\n5262 |         5    False\n5263 |         Name: animal, dtype: bool\n5264 | \n5265 |         Strings and integers are distinct and are therefore not comparable:\n5266 | \n5267 |         >>> pd.Series([1]).isin([\"1\"])\n5268 |         0    False\n5269 |         dtype: bool\n5270 |         >>> pd.Series([1.1]).isin([\"1.1\"])\n5271 |         0    False\n5272 |         dtype: bool\n5273 |         \"\"\"\n5274 |         result = algorithms.isin(self._values, values)\n5275 |         return self._constructor(result, index=self.index, copy=False).__finalize__(\n5276 |             self, method=\"isin\"\n5277 |         )\n```\n\nFound function `asv_bench/benchmarks/timeseries.py:DatetimeAccessor.time_dt_accessor`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n275 | def time_dt_accessor(self, tz):\n276 |         self.series.dt\n```\n\nFound function `asv_bench/benchmarks/timeseries.py:DatetimeAccessor.time_dt_accessor_month_name`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n281 | def time_dt_accessor_month_name(self, tz):\n282 |         self.series.dt.month_name()\n```\n\nFound function `asv_bench/benchmarks/timeseries.py:DatetimeAccessor.time_dt_accessor_time`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n287 | def time_dt_accessor_time(self, tz):\n288 |         self.series.dt.time\n```\n\nFound function 