ll,\n    \"start\": 0,\n    \"limit\": 12\n  },\n  \"artifacts\": [\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"run\",\n      \"path\": \"data point 450/DaLIC/A.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"function\",\n      \"defined_line\": 5,\n      \"end_line\": 12,\n      \"event_count\": 6,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_more\": false,\n  \"next_start\": 1\n}"}, {"content": "Let me also check the implementation of the ExcelWriter class since it's involved in the to_excel functionality:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['pandas/io/excel/_base.py:ExcelWriter']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"pandas/io/excel/_base.py:ExcelWriter\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `pandas/io/excel/_base.py:ExcelWriter`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `pandas/io/excel/_base.py:ExcelWriter`.\n```\n 955 | class ExcelWriter(Generic[_WorkbookT]):\n 956 |     \"\"\"\n 957 |     Class for writing DataFrame objects into excel sheets.\n 958 | \n 959 |     Default is to use:\n 960 | \n 961 |     * `xlsxwriter <https://pypi.org/project/XlsxWriter/>`__ for xlsx files if xlsxwriter\n 962 |       is installed otherwise `openpyxl <https://pypi.org/project/openpyxl/>`__\n 963 |     * `odf <https://pypi.org/project/odfpy/>`__ for ods files\n 964 | \n 965 |     See :meth:`DataFrame.to_excel` for typical usage.\n 966 | \n 967 |     The writer should be used as a context manager. Otherwise, call `close()` to save\n 968 |     and close any opened file handles.\n 969 | \n 970 |     Parameters\n 971 |     ----------\n 972 |     path : str or typing.BinaryIO\n 973 |         Path to xls or xlsx or ods file.\n 974 |     engine : str (optional)\n 975 |         Engine to use for writing. If None, defaults to\n 976 |         ``io.excel.<extension>.writer``.  NOTE: can only be passed as a keyword\n 977 |         argument.\n 978 |     date_format : str, default None\n 979 |         Format string for dates written into Excel files (e.g. 'YYYY-MM-DD').\n 980 |     datetime_format : str, default None\n 981 |         Format string for datetime objects written into Excel files.\n 982 |         (e.g. 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS').\n 983 |     mode : {{'w', 'a'}}, default 'w'\n 984 |         File mode to use (write or append). Append does not work with fsspec URLs.\n 985 |     {storage_options}\n 986 | \n 987 |     if_sheet_exists : {{'error', 'new', 'replace', 'overlay'}}, default 'error'\n 988 |         How to behave when trying to write to a sheet that already\n 989 |         exists (append mode only).\n 990 | \n 991 |         * error: raise a ValueError.\n 992 |         * new: Create a new sheet, with a name determined by the engine.\n 993 |         * replace: Delete the contents of the sheet before writing to it.\n 994 |         * overlay: Write contents to the existing sheet without first removing,\n 995 |           but possibly over top of, the existing contents.\n 996 | \n 997 |         .. versionadded:: 1.3.0\n 998 | \n 999 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0\n1000 | \n1001 |            Added ``overlay`` option\n1002 | \n1003 |     engine_kwargs : dict, optional\n1004 |         Keyword arguments to be passed into the engine. These will be passed to\n1005 |         the following functions of the respective engines:\n1006 | \n1007 |         * xlsxwriter: ``xlsxwriter.Workbook(file, **engine_kwargs)``\n1008 |         * openpyxl (write mode): ``openpyxl.Workbook(**engine_kwargs)``\n1009 |         * openpyxl (append mode): ``openpyxl.load_workbook(file, **engine_kwargs)``\n1010 |         * odf: ``odf.opendocument.OpenDocumentSpreadsheet(**engine_kwargs)``\n1011 | \n1012 |         .. versionadded:: 1.3.0\n1013 | \n1014 |     See Also\n1015 |     --------\n1016 |     read_excel : Read an Excel sheet values (xlsx) file into DataFrame.\n1017 |     read_csv : Read a comma-separated values (csv) file into DataFrame.\n1018 |     read_fwf : Read a table of fixed-width formatted lines into DataFrame.\n1019 | \n1020 |     Notes\n1021 |     -----\n1022 |     For compatibility with CSV writers, ExcelWriter serializes lists\n1023 |     and dicts to strings before writing.\n1024 | \n1025 |     Examples\n1026 |     --------\n1027 |     Default usage:\n1028 | \n1029 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[\"ABC\", \"XYZ\"]], columns=[\"Foo\", \"Bar\"])  # doctest: +SKIP\n1030 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\"path_to_file.xlsx\") as writer:\n1031 |     ...     df.to_excel(writer)  # doctest: +SKIP\n1032 | \n1033 |     To write to separate sheets in a single file:\n1034 | \n1035 |     >>> df1 = pd.DataFrame([[\"AAA\", \"BBB\"]], columns=[\"Spam\", \"Egg\"])  # doctest: +SKIP\n1036 |     >>> df2 = pd.DataFrame([[\"ABC\", \"XYZ\"]], columns=[\"Foo\", \"Bar\"])  # doctest: +SKIP\n1037 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\"path_to_file.xlsx\") as writer:\n1038 |     ...     df1.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet1\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n1039 |     ...     df2.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet2\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n1040 | \n1041 |     You can set the date format or datetime format:\n1042 | \n1043 |     >>> from datetime import date, datetime  # doctest: +SKIP\n1044 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n1045 |     ...     [\n1046 |     ...         [date(2014, 1, 31), date(1999, 9, 24)],\n1047 |     ...         [datetime(1998, 5, 26, 23, 33, 4), datetime(2014, 2, 28, 13, 5, 13)],\n1048 |     ...     ],\n1049 |     ...     index=[\"Date\", \"Datetime\"],\n1050 |     ...     columns=[\"X\", \"Y\"],\n1051 |     ... )  # doctest: +SKIP\n1052 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\n1053 |     ...     \"path_to_file.xlsx\",\n1054 |     ...     date_format=\"YYYY-MM-DD\",\n1055 |     ...     datetime_format=\"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS\",\n1056 |     ... ) as writer:\n1057 |     ...     df.to_excel(writer)  # doctest: +SKIP\n1058 | \n1059 |     You can also append to an existing Excel file:\n1060 | \n1061 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\"path_to_file.xlsx\", mode=\"a\", engine=\"openpyxl\") as writer:\n1062 |     ...     df.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet3\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n1063 | \n1064 |     Here, the `if_sheet_exists` parameter can be set to replace a sheet if it\n1065 |     already exists:\n1066 | \n1067 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\n1068 |     ...     \"path_to_file.xlsx\",\n1069 |     ...     mode=\"a\",\n1070 |     ...     engine=\"openpyxl\",\n1071 |     ...     if_sheet_exists=\"replace\",\n1072 |     ... ) as writer:\n1073 |     ...     df.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet1\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n1074 | \n1075 |     You can also write multiple DataFrames to a single sheet. Note that the\n1076 |     ``if_sheet_exists`` parameter needs to be set to ``overlay``:\n1077 | \n1078 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\n1079 |     ...     \"path_to_file.xlsx\",\n1080 |     ...     mode=\"a\",\n1081 |     ...     engine=\"openpyxl\",\n1082 |     ...     if_sheet_exists=\"overlay\",\n1083 |     ... ) as writer:\n1084 |     ...     df1.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet1\")\n1085 |     ...     df2.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet1\", startcol=3)  # doctest: +SKIP\n1086 | \n1087 |     You can store Excel file in RAM:\n1088 | \n1089 |     >>> import io\n1090 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[\"ABC\", \"XYZ\"]], columns=[\"Foo\", \"Bar\"])\n1091 |     >>> buffer = io.BytesIO()\n1092 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(buffer) as writer:\n1093 |     ...     df.to_excel(writer)\n1094 | \n1095 |     You can pack Excel file into zip archive:\n1096 | \n1097 |     >>> import zipfile  # doctest: +SKIP\n1098 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[\"ABC\", \"XYZ\"]], columns=[\"Foo\", \"Bar\"])  # doctest: +SKIP\n1099 |     >>> with zipfile.ZipFile(\"path_to_file.zip\", \"w\") as zf:\n1100 |     ...     with zf.open(\"filename.xlsx\", \"w\") as buffer:\n1101 |     ...         with pd.ExcelWriter(buffer) as writer:\n1102 |     ...             df.to_excel(writer)  # doctest: +SKIP\n1103 | \n1104 |     You can specify additional arguments to the underlying engine:\n1105 | \n1106 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\n1107 |     ...     \"path_to_file.xlsx\",\n1108 |     ...     engine=\"xlsxwriter\",\n1109 |     ...     engine_kwargs={{\"options\": {{\"nan_inf_to_errors\": True}}}},\n1110 |     ... ) as writer:\n1111 |     ...     df.to_excel(writer)  # doctest: +SKIP\n1112 | \n1113 |     In append mode, ``engine_kwargs`` are passed through to\n1114 |     openpyxl's ``load_workbook``:\n1115 | \n1116 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\n1117 |     ...     \"path_to_file.xlsx\",\n1118 |     ...     engine=\"openpyxl\",\n1119 |     ...     mode=\"a\",\n1120 |     ...     engine_kwargs={{\"keep_vba\": True}},\n1121 |     ... ) as writer:\n1122 |     ...     df.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet2\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n1123 |     \"\"\"\n1124 | \n1125 |     # Defining an ExcelWriter implementation (see abstract methods for more...)\n1126 | \n1127 |     # - Mandatory\n1128 |     #   - ``write_cells(self, cells, sheet_name=None, startrow=0, startcol=0)``\n1129 |     #     --> called to write additional DataFrames to disk\n1130 |     #   - ``_supported_extensions`` (tuple of supported extensions), used to\n1131 |     #      check that engine supports the given extension.\n1132 |     #   - ``_engine`` - string that gives the engine name. Necessary to\n1133 |     #     instantiate class directly and bypass ``ExcelWriterMeta`` engine\n1134 |     #     lookup.\n1135 |     #   - ``save(self)`` --> called to save file to disk\n1136 |     # - Mostly mandatory (i.e. should at least exist)\n1137 |     #   - book, cur_sheet, path\n1138 | \n1139 |     # - Optional:\n1140 |     #   - ``__init__(self, path, engine=None, **kwargs)`` --> always called\n1141 |     #     with path as first argument.\n1142 | \n1143 |     # You also need to register the class with ``register_writer()``.\n1144 |     # Technically, ExcelWriter implementations don't need to subclass\n1145 |     # ExcelWriter.\n1146 | \n1147 |     _engine: str\n1148 |     _supported_extensions: tuple[str, ...]\n1149 | \n1150 |     def __new__(\n1151 |         cls,\n1152 |         path: FilePath | WriteExcelBuffer | ExcelWriter,\n1153 |         engine: str | None = None,\n1154 |         date_format: str | None = None,\n1155 |         datetime_format: str | None = None,\n1156 |         mode: str = \"w\",\n1157 |         storage_options: StorageOptions | None = None,\n1158 |         if_sheet_exists: ExcelWriterIfSheetExists | None = None,\n1159 |         engine_kwargs: dict | None = None,\n1160 |     ) -> Self:\n1161 |         # only switch class if generic(ExcelWriter)\n1162 |         if cls is ExcelWriter:\n1163 |             if engine is None or (isinstance(engine, str) and engine == \"auto\"):\n1164 |                 if isinstance(path, str):\n1165 |                     ext = os.path.splitext(path)[-1][1:]\n1166 |                 else:\n1167 |                     ext = \"xlsx\"\n1168 | \n1169 |                 try:\n1170 |                     engine = config.get_option(f\"io.excel.{ext}.writer\")\n1171 |                     if engine == \"auto\":\n1172 |                         engine = get_default_engine(ext, mode=\"writer\")\n1173 |                 except KeyError as err:\n1174 |                     raise ValueError(f\"No engine for filetype: '{ext}'\") from err\n1175 | \n1176 |             # for mypy\n1177 |             assert engine is not None\n1178 |             #  error: Incompatible types in assignment (expression has type\n1179 |             #  \"type[ExcelWriter[Any]]\", variable has type \"type[Self]\")\n1180 |             cls = get_writer(engine)  # type: ignore[assignment]\n1181 | \n1182 |         return object.__new__(cls)\n1183 | \n1184 |     # declare external properties you can count on\n1185 |     _path = None\n1186 | \n1187 |     @property\n1188 |     def supported_extensions(self) -> tuple[str, ...]:\n1189 |         \"\"\"Extensions that writer engine supports.\"\"\"\n1190 |         return self._supported_extensions\n1191 | \n1192 |     @property\n1193 |     def engine(self) -> str:\n1194 |         \"\"\"Name of engine.\"\"\"\n1195 |         return self._engine\n1196 | \n1197 |     @property\n1198 |     def sheets(self) -> dict[str, Any]:\n1199 |         \"\"\"Mapping of sheet names to sheet objects.\"\"\"\n1200 |         raise NotImplementedError\n1201 | \n1202 |     @property\n1203 |     def book(self) -> _WorkbookT:\n1204 |         \"\"\"\n1205 |         Book instance. Class type will depend on the engine used.\n1206 | \n1207 |         This attribute can be used to access engine-specific features.\n1208 |         \"\"\"\n1209 |         raise NotImplementedError\n1210 | \n1211 |     def _write_cells(\n1212 |         self,\n1213 |         cells,\n1214 |         sheet_name: str | None = None,\n1215 |         startrow: int = 0,\n1216 |         startcol: int = 0,\n1217 |         freeze_panes: tuple[int, int] | None = None,\n1218 |     ) -> None:\n1219 |         \"\"\"\n1220 |         Write given formatted cells into Excel an excel sheet\n1221 | \n1222 |         Parameters\n1223 |         ----------\n1224 |         cells : generator\n1225 |             cell of formatted data to save to Excel sheet\n1226 |         sheet_name : str, default None\n1227 |             Name of Excel sheet, if None, then use self.cur_sheet\n1228 |         startrow : upper left cell row to dump data frame\n1229 |         startcol : upper left cell column to dump data frame\n1230 |         freeze_panes: int tuple of length 2\n1231 |             contains the bottom-most row and right-most column to freeze\n1232 |         \"\"\"\n1233 |         raise NotImplementedError\n1234 | \n1235 |     def _save(self) -> None:\n1236 |         \"\"\"\n1237 |         Save workbook to disk.\n1238 |         \"\"\"\n1239 |         raise NotImplementedError\n1240 | \n1241 |     def __init__(\n1242 |         self,\n1243 |         path: FilePath | WriteExcelBuffer | ExcelWriter,\n1244 |         engine: str | None = None,\n1245 |         date_format: str | None = None,\n1246 |         datetime_format: str | None = None,\n1247 |         mode: str = \"w\",\n1248 |         storage_options: StorageOptions | None = None,\n1249 |         if_sheet_exists: ExcelWriterIfSheetExists | None = None,\n1250 |         engine_kwargs: dict[str, Any] | None = None,\n1251 |     ) -> None:\n1252 |         # validate that this engine can handle the extension\n1253 |         if isinstance(path, str):\n1254 |             ext = os.path.splitext(path)[-1]\n1255 |             self.check_extension(ext)\n1256 | \n1257 |         # use mode to open the file\n1258 |         if \"b\" not in mode:\n1259 |             mode += \"b\"\n1260 |         # use \"a\" for the user to append data to excel but internally use \"r+\" to let\n1261 |         # the excel backend first read the existing file and then write any data to it\n1262 |         mode = mode.replace(\"a\", \"r+\")\n1263 | \n1264 |         if if_sheet_exists not in (None, \"error\", \"new\", \"replace\", \"overlay\"):\n1265 |             raise ValueError(\n1266 |                 f\"'{if_sheet_exists}' is not valid for if_sheet_exists. \"\n1267 |                 \"Valid options are 'error', 'new', 'replace' and 'overlay'.\"\n1268 |             )\n1269 |         if if_sheet_exists and \"r+\" not in mode:\n1270 |             raise ValueError(\"if_sheet_exists is only valid in append mode (mode='a')\")\n1271 |         if if_sheet_exists is None:\n1272 |             if_sheet_exists = \"error\"\n1273 |         self._if_sheet_exists = if_sheet_exists\n1274 | \n1275 |         # cast ExcelWriter to avoid adding 'if self._handles is not None'\n1276 |         self._handles = IOHandles(\n1277 |             cast(IO[bytes], path), compression={\"compression\": None}\n1278 |         )\n1279 |         if not isinstance(path, ExcelWriter):\n1280 |             self._handles = get_handle(\n1281 |                 path, mode, storage_options=storage_options, is_text=False\n1282 |             )\n1283 |         self._cur_sheet = None\n1284 | \n1285 |         if date_format is None:\n1286 |             self._date_format = \"YYYY-MM-DD\"\n1287 |         else:\n1288 |             self._date_format = date_format\n1289 |         if datetime_format is None:\n1290 |             self._datetime_format = \"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS\"\n1291 |         else:\n1292 |             self._datetime_format = datetime_format\n1293 | \n1294 |         self._mode = mode\n1295 | \n1296 |     @property\n1297 |     def date_format(self) -> str:\n1298 |         \"\"\"\n1299 |         Format string for dates written into Excel files (e.g. 'YYYY-MM-DD').\n1300 |         \"\"\"\n1301 |         return self._date_format\n1302 | \n1303 |     @property\n1304 |     def datetime_format(self) -> str:\n1305 |         \"\"\"\n1306 |         Format string for dates written into Excel files (e.g. 'YYYY-MM-DD').\n1307 |         \"\"\"\n1308 |         return self._datetime_format\n1309 | \n1310 |     @property\n1311 |     def if_sheet_exists(self) -> str:\n1312 |         \"\"\"\n1313 |         How to behave when writing to a sheet that already exists in append mode.\n1314 |         \"\"\"\n1315 |         return self._if_sheet_exists\n1316 | \n1317 |     def __fspath__(self) -> str:\n1318 |         return getattr(self._handles.handle, \"name\", \"\")\n1319 | \n1320 |     def _get_sheet_name(self, sheet_name: str | None) -> str:\n1321 |         if sheet_name is None:\n1322 |             sheet_name = self._cur_sheet\n1323 |         if sheet_name is None:  # pragma: no cover\n1324 |             raise ValueError(\"Must pass explicit sheet_name or set _cur_sheet property\")\n1325 |         return sheet_name\n1326 | \n1327 |     def _value_with_fmt(\n1328 |         self, val\n1329 |     ) -> tuple[\n1330 |         int | float | bool | str | datetime.datetime | datetime.date, str | None\n1331 |     ]:\n1332 |         \"\"\"\n1333 |         Convert numpy types to Python types for the Excel writers.\n1334 | \n1335 |         Parameters\n1336 |         ----------\n1337 |         val : object\n1338 |             Value to be written into cells\n1339 | \n1340 |         Returns\n1341 |         -------\n1342 |         Tuple with the first element being the converted value and the second\n1343 |             being an optional format\n1344 |         \"\"\"\n1345 |         fmt = None\n1346 | \n1347 |         if is_integer(val):\n1348 |             val = int(val)\n1349 |         elif is_float(val):\n1350 |             val = float(val)\n1351 |         elif is_bool(val):\n1352 |             val = bool(val)\n1353 |         elif is_decimal(val):\n1354 |             val = Decimal(val)\n1355 |         elif isinstance(val, datetime.datetime):\n1356 |             fmt = self._datetime_format\n1357 |         elif isinstance(val, datetime.date):\n1358 |             fmt = self._date_format\n1359 |         elif isinstance(val, datetime.timedelta):\n1360 |             val = val.total_seconds() / 86400\n1361 |             fmt = \"0\"\n1362 |         else:\n1363 |             val = str(val)\n1364 |             # GH#56954\n1365 |             # Excel's limitation on cell contents is 32767 characters\n1366 |             # xref https://support.microsoft.com/en-au/office/excel-specifications-and-limits-1672b34d-7043-467e-8e27-269d656771c3\n1367 |             if len(val) > 32767:\n1368 |                 warnings.warn(\n1369 |                     f\"Cell contents too long ({len(val)}), \"\n1370 |                     \"truncated to 32767 characters\",\n1371 |                     UserWarning,\n1372 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n1373 |                 )\n1374 |         return val, fmt\n1375 | \n1376 |     @classmethod\n1377 |     def check_extension(cls, ext: str) -> Literal[True]:\n1378 |         \"\"\"\n1379 |         checks that path's extension against the Writer's supported\n1380 |         extensions.  If it isn't supported, raises UnsupportedFiletypeError.\n1381 |         \"\"\"\n1382 |         if ext.startswith(\".\"):\n1383 |             ext = ext[1:]\n1384 |         if not any(ext in extension for extension in cls._supported_extensions):\n1385 |             raise ValueError(f\"Invalid extension for engine '{cls.engine}': '{ext}'\")\n1386 |         return True\n1387 | \n1388 |     # Allow use as a contextmanager\n1389 |     def __enter__(self) -> Self:\n1390 |         return self\n1391 | \n1392 |     def __exit__(\n1393 |         self,\n1394 |         exc_type: type[BaseException] | None,\n1395 |         exc_value: BaseException | None,\n1396 |         traceback: TracebackType | None,\n1397 |     ) -> None:\n1398 |         self.close()\n1399 | \n1400 |     def close(self) -> None:\n1401 |         \"\"\"synonym for save, to make it more file-like\"\"\"\n1402 |         self._save()\n1403 |         self._handles.close()\n```\n"}, {"content": "After reviewing all the findings and checking for additional references, I can confirm that the previously identified locations are complete and sufficient to address the documentation update. Here's why:\n\n1. Main Implementation Location:\n   - The core implementation of the merge_cells functionality is in the ExcelFormatter class (pandas/io/formats/excel.py), which was already correctly identified. This class handles both the logic for multi-index columns and hierarchical rows merging.\n\n2. Documentation Location:\n   - The issue specifically requests documentation updates for the merge_cells parameter in the DataFrame.to_excel() method.\n   - The primary documentation file that needs updating is doc/source/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.to_excel.rst\n\n3. Supporting Evidence:\n   - The ExcelWriter class (pandas/io/excel/_base.py) shows the relationship between pandas and the underlying Excel writing libraries.\n   - The implementation in ExcelFormatter shows that the \"columns\" option is valid for both MultiIndex columns and hierarchical rows.\n   - The reference to version 3.0.0 in the implementation confirms this feature was added in that version.\n\n4. Dependencies:\n   - The change is isolated to documentation and doesn't affect the core implementation.\n   - No changes to the underlying Excel writing logic or dependencies are required.\n   - The implementation in ExcelFormatter correctly handles the merging logic for both general hierarchical rows and specific column merging cases.\n\nBased on this verification, I can now provide the final, location-only answer in the requested format:\n\n```\ndoc/source/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.to_excel.rst\nline: relative to be updated documentation file\n```\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nYour last answer did not follow the required final output format.\n\nRewrite your final answer now using this exact structure:\n\n```\npath/to/file1.py\nline: 10-20\nclass: MyClass\nfunction: my_function\n\npath/to/file2.py\nline: 42\nfunction: another_function\n```\n<finish></finish>\n\nRules:\n- Output ONLY one fenced code block, then `<finish></finish>`\n- Each file path must be on its own line and end with `.py`\n- Do NOT use `file.py:QualifiedName` on one line\n- Do NOT include explanations, headings, bullets, numbering, or any prose\n- Return just the location(s)\n"}, {"content": "```\ndoc/source/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.to_excel.rst\nline: relative to be updated documentation file\n```\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nYour last answer did not follow the required final output format.\n\nRewrite your final answer now using this exact structure:\n\n```\npath/to/file1.py\nline: 10-20\nclass: MyClass\nfunction: my_function\n\npath/to/file2.py\nline: 42\nfunction: another_function\n```\n<finish></finish>\n\nRules:\n- Output ONLY one fenced code block, then `<finish></finish>`\n- Each file path must be on its own line and end with `.py`\n- Do NOT use `file.py:QualifiedName` on one line\n- Do NOT include explanations, headings, bullets, numbering, or any prose\n- Return just the location(s)\n"}, {"content": "```\npandas/core/generic.py\nline: 2214-2216\nclass: NDFrame\nfunction: to_excel\n\npandas/io/formats/excel.py\nline: 527-528\nclass: ExcelFormatter\nfunction: __init__\n\npandas/io/formats/excel.py\nline: 633-634\nclass: ExcelFormatter\nfunction: _format_header_mi\n\npandas/io/formats/excel.py\nline: 803-805\nclass: ExcelFormatter\nfunction: _format_hierarchical_rows\n\ndoc/source/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.to_excel.rst\nline: relative to be updated documentation file\n```\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_data_dependencies", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n- '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.  \n- It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\nArgs:\n- `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names. \n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes. \n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`. \n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope. \n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n- `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. \n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n- `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols. \n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n\nImportant behavior:\n- If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n- If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n- `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n- `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n- `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n- `symbol`: The symbol name.\n- `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n- `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n\nEach edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n- `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n- `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n- During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"symbol_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.", "default": null}, "scope_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.", "default": null}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.", "default": null}, "edge_type": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 12, "description": "Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice."}, "path_limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 4, "description": "Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 586624, "completion_tokens": 1160}, "time": 297.01054787635803}]}}
